076171875, -0.0304412841796875, -0.0077362060546875, -0.0175018310546875, 0.006805419921875, 0.027374267578125, -0.006404876708984375, -0.007480621337890625, 0.01560211181640625, -0.01435089111328125, -0.01320648193359375, 0.0404052734375, 0.005706787109375, 0.044891357421875, -0.002376556396484375, 0.00945281982421875, -0.0218963623046875, 0.0298004150390625, 0.01558685302734375, 0.00334930419921875, 0.004093170166015625, -0.0224609375, -0.0123748779296875, 0.00959014892578125, 0.0033397674560546875, -0.015777587890625, 0.033843994140625, -0.0002949237823486328, 0.0260162353515625, -0.0157318115234375, -0.0012483596801757812, -0.0152740478515625, 0.007083892822265625, 0.0205535888671875, -0.0149078369140625, 0.013427734375, -0.004161834716796875, -0.0250701904296875, 0.0298919677734375, -0.023773193359375, -0.026824951171875, 0.0199127197265625, 0.0036525726318359375, 0.01242828369140625, 0.00402069091796875, 0.0272674560546875, 0.04278564453125, -0.01352691650390625, -0.01436614990234375, -0.003078460693359375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose  \nTo develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods  \n\nStudy Design and Data  \nThis retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists at a single institution in 2023 and 2024.\n\nMulti-Agent AI Pipeline Architecture  \nThe system comprised two primary agent types orchestrated in a sequential pipeline. First, a rule-based agent used regular expressions (regex) to identify and segment sentences into standardized anatomical sections (e.g., lungs, liver, kidneys). For example, regex patterns such as `r\"\\b(lungs?|pulmonary)\\b\"` and `r\"\\b(liver|hepatic)\\b\"` were used to match section headers and anatomical terms. These patterns were iteratively developed by reviewing a random sample of 100 local reports, adding or refining expressions to capture institution-specific section headers and common synonyms. The criteria for iterative development included maximizing recall for anatomical section identification while minimizing false positives, as assessed by manual review of regex outputs on a validation set of 50 reports.\n\nSecond, a local large language model (LLM) agent\u2014specifically, a 7B-parameter Llama-2 model running on-premises\u2014was used to resolve ambiguous or complex sentence assignments and to perform semantic checks. The LLM was not fine-tuned on local data but was prompted with institution-specific instructions. Sample prompts included: \u201cAssign the following sentence to the most appropriate anatomical section based on the provided section list: [sentence]. Use local reporting conventions as described: [list of conventions].\u201d Prompt engineering involved iterative testing of prompt templates on a validation set, with radiologist feedback to ensure compatibility with local reporting styles. The LLM\u2019s outputs were evaluated for accuracy and consistency by comparing them to expert-annotated section assignments.\n\nThe agents interacted via a controller script: the regex agent parsed the report and generated a preliminary section assignment, which was then reviewed and refined by the LLM agent. The LLM also performed quality assurance (QA) checks on the structured output. This hybrid approach leveraged the precision of rule-based parsing for well-structured content and the flexibility of LLMs for handling variability in reporting language.\n\nQuality Assurance Tasks  \nThe system flagged reports for three predefined QA tasks: (a) mismatches between Findings and Impression sections or within sections (e.g., findings present in one section but not reflected in the Impression), (b) gender-anatomy conflicts (e.g., references to male anatomy in female patients), and (c) undocumented communication of critical findings (e.g., critical results not documented as communicated). Each QA task was implemented as a separate module within the LLM agent, using prompt-based logic to identify relevant discrepancies. For example, the gender-anatomy module used prompts such as: \u201cDoes the report mention anatomical findings inconsistent with the patient\u2019s gender?\u201d The critical findings module used: \u201cIs there mention of a critical finding, and if so, is communication of this finding documented?\u201d\n\nEvaluation Design and Metrics  \nA random subset of 45 reports was selected for radiologist evaluation. The sample size was chosen to provide a 95% confidence interval of \u00b115% for the primary outcome (correct restructuring rate), assuming an expected accuracy of 70%. This assumption was based on pilot data from an initial set of 20 reports, which showed a correct restructuring rate of 65\u201375%. The sample size calculation followed the formula for estimating a proportion with specified confidence and precision (see, e.g., Lwanga & Lemeshow, \u201cSample Size Determination in Health Studies,\u201d WHO, 1991). Stratification ensured balanced representation: 23 flagged reports (across all QA tasks) and 22 unflagged reports were randomly sampled to reflect the overall flagged/unflagged ratio.\n\nTwo board-certified radiologists independently reviewed each report and assessed: (1) correctness of restructuring, defined as accurate anatomical sectioning with preservation of original content, no omission or fabrication, and correct assignment of all sentences to appropriate sections; (2) QA performance for each flagged issue (rated as true positive, false positive, true negative, or false negative for each QA task); and (3) whether any clinically important information was omitted or fabricated. Disagreements were resolved by consensus discussion: both reviewers independently annotated each report, then met to review all disagreements, discussing each case until consensus was reached. If consensus could not be reached, a third radiologist arbitrated, but this was not required in this study. Inter-rater agreement was measured using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic.\n\nSystem Requirements and Deployment  \nThe system was deployed on a local workstation with dual NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPUs, 256 GB RAM, and Ubuntu 22.04. The LLM inference time averaged 2.5 seconds per report, and the regex agent processed each report in under 0.1 seconds. Integration into clinical workflow was not performed; the system operated as a standalone batch processor. Barriers to real-world deployment include hardware requirements for LLM inference, lack of integration with radiology information systems, and the need for further validation.\n\nResults  \n\nReport Structuring  \nThe system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format, retaining original content. Of the 45 evaluated reports, both reviewers agreed that 31 (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and 12 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%) had reviewer disagreement. Correct restructuring required that all sentences were assigned to the correct anatomical section, with no content omitted or fabricated. Cohen\u2019s kappa for restructuring correctness was 0.62, indicating substantial agreement.\n\nQuality Assurance Task Performance  \nDetailed confusion matrices for each QA task are shown below:\n\n| QA Task                  | True Pos | False Pos | True Neg | False Neg | Sensitivity | Specificity |\n|--------------------------|----------|-----------|----------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| Section Mismatch         |   10     |     2     |   31     |     2     |    0.83     |    0.95     |\n| Gender-Anatomy Conflict  |    2     |     0     |   43     |     0     |    1.00     |    1.00     |\n| Undocumented Critical    |    1     |     0     |   44     |     0     |    1.00     |    1.00     |\n\nFor section mismatches, most false positives (2/14 flagged) were due to ambiguous language or non-standard section headers. False negatives (2/12 actual mismatches) occurred when findings were described in non-standard phrasing that the LLM failed to map correctly. For gender-anatomy conflicts and undocumented critical findings, all flagged cases were true positives.\n\nAnalysis of Flagged Errors  \nOf the 90 reports flagged in the full dataset, 80 (12.5%) were for section mismatches, 7 (1.1%) for gender-anatomy conflicts, and 3 (0.5%) for undocumented critical findings. Section mismatches were categorized as follows: 54 (67.5%) involved omission of benign findings from the Impression, 14 (17.5%) involved omission of potentially clinically significant findings (e.g., pulmonary nodule, liver lesion), and 12 (15%) involved misassignment of findings to incorrect anatomical sections. Sources of error included language ambiguity (38%), atypical or missing section headers (27%), and template errors in dictation (15%). The remaining errors were due to rare anatomical variants or complex sentence structure (20%). The potential clinical impact was quantified: 12/80 section mismatches (15%) involved findings that, if omitted from the Impression, could affect clinical decision-making. Gender-anatomy conflict errors were rare and typically resulted from template errors; none were clinically significant. All undocumented critical findings flagged were confirmed as true positives, but the small number precludes robust conclusions.\n\nLimitations  \nThis study is limited by its retrospective design, single-institution data, and potential overfitting to local reporting styles, particularly due to iterative regex development on local reports. This may limit generalizability and introduce spectrum bias, as the system may perform differently on reports from other institutions or with different reporting conventions. The use of only board-certified radiologists from the same institution for both report generation and evaluation introduces possible evaluator bias and may inflate agreement rates. The modest sample size for radiologist evaluation limits statistical power and may not capture the full range of reporting variability. The lack of comparison to existing structuring or QA methods precludes assessment of relative performance, and the absence of prospective deployment or integration into clinical workflow limits conclusions about real-world impact. No external reviewers were included, and the system\u2019s computational requirements may pose barriers to broader adoption. These limitations restrict the interpretation of results and preclude confirmatory conclusions.\n\nConclusion  \nA locally deployed multi-agent AI system combining rule-based and LLM agents enabled radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow. In a limited, single-institution radiologist evaluation, the system demonstrated preliminary performance, with moderate accuracy for report restructuring and high sensitivity and specificity for most QA tasks. These findings are hypothesis-generating and require further validation\u2014including comparison to existing methods, prospective deployment, and multi-institutional studies with external reviewers\u2014to confirm generalizability and clinical utility. Such systems may support standardization of reporting and quality assurance in radiology practice, but current findings should be interpreted as preliminary and not as evidence of clinical effectiveness."}}